# Summary

This vignette examines how different conventions for handling negative
growth-rate measurements affect concentration-response modelling and the
resulting toxicity estimates. Negative growth rates are common in algal toxicity
tests and other ecological endpoints that measure change over time, yet many
analytical workflows modify or remove these observations before model fitting.

Using simulated datasets where the true toxicity values are known, six commonly
used approaches were compared: retaining measured values, censoring values below
zero, deleting negative observations, replacing negatives with zero
("flooring"), and fitting Beta or Gamma response distributions that do not
permit negative values. Performance was assessed for ErC10, ErC50 and NSEC
estimates across a range of experimental designs and levels of measurement
precision.

The results show that approaches which retain the measured information, either
directly or through censoring, consistently recover the true toxicity values,
maintain nominal interval coverage, and improve as experimental precision
increases. Deleting negative observations performs less well but remains largely
unbiased. In contrast, conventions that replace negative values or impose a
non-negative response distribution introduce systematic bias that does not
disappear with increasing precision. Flooring negative values produces
increasingly over-conservative toxicity estimates, particularly for the NSEC,
while Beta and Gamma models can introduce substantial bias despite successfully
converging.

Application to four real algal growth datasets demonstrated that the choice of
convention can materially affect reported toxicity values, although the
magnitude of the effect depends on the prevalence of observations at or below
zero growth. The simulation results indicate that the direction of the bias is
predictable even when its magnitude is dataset specific.

Overall, the study supports analysing growth-rate data as measured whenever
possible and using censoring when values below a detection or counting limit are
unavailable. The results provide strong evidence against replacing negative
values with zero or selecting bounded response distributions solely because
negative responses are present. These practices distort the data-generating
process and can produce toxicity estimates that become more confidently wrong as
data quality improves.

# 1 Negative growth rates

Some ecotoxicological responses can legitimately take negative values. Specific
growth rate is the most common example: as a rate of change, it is negative for
a population that declines over the course of a test. Other examples include
increments, differences between paired measurements, and any response expressed
as a change from a baseline.

However, these negative values are typically removed before fitting, both in
this package and in others. The reasons are long-standing, and none of
them is specific to any one piece of software. A negative value cannot be
expressed as a percentage of the control without the percentage exceeding 100,
and several commonly used concentration-response equations cannot generate a
negative mean at all. In addition, a negative growth rate looks anomalous on a
plot, which may cause the reader to treat it as an error and not as a
measurement.

The negative values are removed in one of several ways: by deleting the rows
that hold them, by replacing them with zero before fitting, by declaring them
censored at zero, or by fitting a response distribution that has no support
below zero (e.g. a Beta or a Gamma). In a minority of cases the negative
values are kept and the model is fitted to the data as recorded, which is the
`measured` convention of Section 5.

Here we examine how each of these conventions affects the toxicity estimate that
is subsequently reported. We first define the estimates and explain why their
definition is important when the response can cross zero (Section 2), and then
set out what replacing a negative value commits the analyst to, which is more
than an edit to the data (Section 3). We also list the equations `bayesnec` fits
under a Gaussian family, and the ECx that `ecx()` returns for them (Section 4).
The conventions are then evaluated in a simulation study in which the true
values are known, so that each convention can be scored against the truth and
not merely compared with the others (Section 5), and the comparison is repeated
on four real algal growth tests (Section 6). The recommendations and the
limitations are given in Sections 7 and 8, and the provenance of each figure
quoted is recorded in Section 9.

To run this vignette we will need the following packages.

```{r, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}
library(bayesnec)
library(brms)
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
```

# 2 Toxicity estimates and their reference

## 2.1 Growth rate and the ErCx notation

In OECD terminology the *endpoint* is the measured biological response, and OECD
Test Guideline 201 [@oecd2026tg201] defines two endpoints from one set of cell
counts and requires both to be reported: the average specific *growth rate*
(i.e. the rate constant of population increase) and the *yield* (i.e. the
biomass increase over the test). The `r` in ErC50 comes from TG 201 and names
the endpoint from which the estimate was derived, so that ErC50 is the EC50 for
the growth rate and EyC50 is the EC50 for the yield (TG 201 writes this letter
as a subscript, whereas we write it inline). For a given dataset EyC50 is
systematically lower than ErC50, and the guideline requires the growth rate to
be reported because it does not depend on test duration.

An ECx or an NSEC (both defined below) is therefore a *toxicity estimate*, not
an endpoint. This distinction is important here because it is the growth-rate
endpoint, and not the toxicity estimate, that can take negative values.
Everything below concerns ErCx.

## 2.2 The ECx reference

`bayesnec` measures each ECx from the control, which is the predicted mean at
the lowest concentration in the supplied predictor, taken per posterior draw.
The `type` argument names what the percentage is measured *towards*:
`"absolute"` (the default) measures from the control to zero, `"relative"` from
the control to the equation's lower asymptote, and `"range"` from the control
to the lowest response the curve predicts, whereas `"direct"` reads the
concentration off a nominated response value (see `?ecx` for the full
definitions).

TG 201 uses the definition given by `type = "absolute"`, under which a 50%
effect means the growth rate has been halved relative to the control, and a
100% effect means growth has stopped altogether. This definition has two
consequences for a response that can go negative. First, zero growth is 100%
effect for any dataset, whatever the control growth rate, so a curve whose
lower asymptote is negative descends past 100% effect and continues to decline.
Second, effects above 100% are meaningful and are not truncated (`ecx_val` is
not capped), because 120% inhibition of a growth rate is a rate of -0.2 times
the control, which is a real measurement on an unbounded response and is the
value reported under TG 201.

ErC10 and ErC50 are always located where the curve is above zero, because they
require the response to be 90% and 50% of the control respectively, and both of
these values are strictly positive. The negative part of the curve can
therefore affect these estimates only indirectly, by changing the shape of the
fitted curve through the observations at high concentrations, and it is this
indirect effect that we quantify in the rest of this vignette.

We do not normalise the response to the control anywhere in this vignette. The
absolute ECx already expresses the effect relative to the fitted control
response, so dividing the data by the control mean beforehand would apply the
same correction twice, and would also distort the error structure [@Ritz2026].

## 2.3 The NSEC

The NSEC is the no-significant-effect-concentration of @fisherfox2023, i.e. the
highest concentration at which the modelled mean response is still
statistically indistinguishable from the modelled control response (read up the
concentration series, this is the point at which the response first becomes
distinguishable from the control). To estimate it, `bayesnec` takes a lower
quantile of the control posterior (by default the 1st percentile, controlled by
`sig_val`) and finds where each posterior draw of the curve crosses it. The
NSEC is one of the estimates recommended for guideline derivation under the
current Australian and New Zealand technical guidance [@warne2025], and its
relationship to the NEC is described by @fisher2023ieam.

The NSEC depends on the width of the control posterior, which determines how
the results below need to be read. This dependence follows from how the metric
is defined, and not from how any approach fits the data. A noisier experiment
tends to produce a wider control posterior, and hence a lower reference value
and a crossing further to the right, at a higher concentration. Conversely, as
the residual error among the control replicates tends to zero, the reference
converges on the control mean and the crossing is found at a lower
concentration.

Several equations in `bayesnec` include a `nec` parameter (a threshold
concentration below which the modelled mean response is flat at the control
level and above which it declines), so that the curve consists of two straight
or curved segments meeting at a corner instead of a single smooth decline. For
such an equation the NSEC converges on `nec` itself as the residual error
falls. The true `nec` is therefore the limiting value of the NSEC, and not its
expected value at any finite level of noise.

# 3 Flooring and the response distribution

Replacing a negative growth rate with zero is more than an edit to the data,
because it asserts that the response cannot be negative. This is a statement
about the response distribution, and the rest of the analysis then has to be
made consistent with it.

However, no available distribution family represents that assertion exactly. A
floored response has a point mass at zero, whereas the distributions available
for a non-negative continuous response exclude zero itself (a Beta is defined on
the open interval (0, 1) and a Gamma on the open interval (0, infinity)). The
floored values are therefore outside the support of the family that the
flooring implies. A distribution that does represent a continuous non-negative
response with a point mass at zero (e.g. a Tweedie) is not available natively
in `brms`.

`bayesnec` accommodates this by shifting the values inside the support of the
Beta or Gamma family. `check_data()` shifts an exact zero to one tenth of the
smallest positive value and, under a Beta, an exact one to `1 - 0.001`.
`bnec()` gives one message for each substitution, naming the number of rows,
the value substituted and the reason, and, for a substituted zero, the family
that would represent a genuine zero instead of shifting it. This accommodation is
a device for making an inconsistent specification runnable, and not a model of
the response.

Substituting zeros and choosing a bounded family are therefore one practice
applied at different stages of the analysis, which is why we do not
treat them as alternatives. The `floored` convention below replaces the
negative values and keeps the family unbounded, whereas the `beta` and `gamma`
conventions make this assertion through the family as well as through the
data.

Two cases are outside this argument. A row whose value was never recorded,
because the population fell below a counting limit, is a censored observation
and not a floored one, and is exempt from the substitution. A response that is
genuinely zero because the population died is a different process again, for
which the hurdle and zero-inflated families are designed (see the [Hurdle and
zero-inflated concentration-response models][e6] vignette). However, these
families are not a remedy for a substituted negative.

# 4 Zero-asymptote equations under a Gaussian family

The interval within which a response distribution allows the mean to fall is a
property of the distribution, and not of the link function. The mean of a
Gaussian distribution is unconstrained, because the data enter the likelihood
only through the residual, and a negative fitted mean is therefore an ordinary
prediction and not an invalid one.

For this reason the six declining equations whose lower asymptote is fixed at
zero (`nec3param`, `ecxexp`, `ecxsigm`, `ecxwb1p3`, `ecxwb2p3` and `ecxll3`) are
retained under a Gaussian family, although none of them can predict a negative
mean while `top` is positive. An absolute ECx is reported for each of them,
even though none has a `bot` parameter. Under a Gaussian family the declining
candidate set that `bnec()` fits by default includes these six and the eight
other declining equations and, together with the constant equation
`ecxflat` (which is also in the `"decline"` group), consists of fifteen
equations.

```{r}
models()$decline
```

In practice, this means that the curve shape described for algal growth-rate
data by both TG 201 and @Ritz2026 (a lower asymptote fixed at zero, representing
complete inhibition) can be fitted directly to the measurements as recorded, and
can be weighed against the shapes with a free lower asymptote by the same model
averaging that `bayesnec` applies to everything else. Whether that shape is used
then depends on the weight the data give it, and not on a choice made in the
model specification. The decision to replace the measurements is a separate one,
made before any equation is fitted, and it is this decision that we examine in
the remaining sections.

# 5 Simulation study

Real datasets can show that the conventions disagree, but not which of them is
correct, because the true toxicity of a real substance is unknown. We therefore
make the comparison on simulated data, for which the true ErC10, ErC50 and `nec`
are known by construction, so that each convention can be scored against them.

## 5.1 The conventions compared

We compare six conventions, which differ only in what is done to the data before
fitting and in the response distribution that is then used.

| name | what the analyst does |
|---|---|
| `measured` | use the growth rates as recorded |
| `censored` | declare each negative value left-censored at zero |
| `deleted` | delete the rows holding a negative value |
| `floored` | replace each negative value with zero |
| `beta` | floor, scale the response to (0, 1], fit a Beta |
| `gamma` | floor, fit a Gamma |

Each convention then calls `bnec()` with the package defaults, i.e. the default
priors derived from its own prepared data, the default declining candidate set
for its family, and the default model averaging over that set (pseudo-BMA with a
Bayesian bootstrap, which `bnec()` uses when `loo_controls` is not supplied). No
convention is given a prior or an equation chosen by another convention. The
difference between two conventions is therefore the difference a practitioner
would obtain by following each convention through, and not a contrast in the
likelihood only, with everything else held artificially fixed.

If the equation were held fixed (e.g. at `nec4param`), any difference could be
attributed only to the treatment of the data. However, this answers a
question about the likelihood, and not the question a reader has, which is what
happens to the number they will report. A fixed equation would also introduce an
artefact, because on altered data a fixed `nec4param` is no longer the equation
that generated them, and part of what it measured would then be
misspecification that the convention did not cause.

The `measured` convention uses the growth rates as recorded, with the lower
asymptote left free wherever the equation has one, and provides the reference
for all of the results below.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
bnec(y ~ crf(x, "decline"), data = dat, family = gaussian(link = "identity"))
```

The `censored` convention declares each negative value censored at zero, so
that the model is told the observation is at or below zero without being told
its value. This is the option available when the negative values were never
recorded. Censoring also changes which observations constrain the mean most
strongly, because a censored row contributes the probability that the response
is at or below the bound, and not a density (see the *Censoring* section of the
[Single model usage][e1] vignette).

```{r, eval = FALSE}
dat_censored <- dat |>
  mutate(cens = ifelse(y < 0, "left", "none"),
         y = ifelse(y < 0, 0, y))
bnec(y | cens(cens) ~ crf(x, "decline"), data = dat_censored,
     family = gaussian(link = "identity"))
```

The `deleted` convention drops the rows holding a negative value and analyses
the remaining data unchanged. Nothing false is asserted about any observation
that is kept. However, the replication per concentration can change, and a
whole treatment group may disappear at the top of the series (i.e. at the
highest concentrations).

```{r, eval = FALSE}
bnec(y ~ crf(x, "decline"), data = filter(dat, y >= 0),
     family = gaussian(link = "identity"))
```

The `floored` convention replaces each negative growth rate with zero and does
not change the model.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
dat_floored <- mutate(dat, y = pmax(y, 0))
bnec(y ~ crf(x, "decline"), data = dat_floored,
     family = gaussian(link = "identity"))
```

The `beta` convention divides the floored response by its maximum, so that it
is in the interval (0, 1], and fits a Beta. Dividing by the maximum is how these
data have been prepared in the work this vignette draws on, whereas dividing by
the control mean is at least as common elsewhere, and @Ritz2026 set out why the
response should not be divided by either before fitting.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
dat_beta <- dat |>
  mutate(y = pmax(y, 0)) |>
  mutate(y = y / max(y))
bnec(y ~ crf(x, "decline"), data = dat_beta, family = Beta(link = "identity"))
```

The `gamma` convention applies no scaling, and fits a Gamma to the floored
response.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
bnec(y ~ crf(x, "decline"), data = dat_floored,
     family = Gamma(link = "identity"))
```

Under the `beta` and `gamma` conventions the zeros created by flooring are
shifted off the boundary by `check_data()` (Section 3), and this shift is part
of the convention under test, not something the study pre-empts. Dividing by
the maximum does not affect the reported toxicity estimates, because
`ecx(type = "absolute")` solves `f(x) = control * (1 - x/100)`. A positive
scaling of the response therefore applies to both sides of this equation, and
the concentration at which the curve meets the target is unchanged. The choice
of family changes the fitted curve, but not the definition of the estimate.

The candidate set differs between families, because the declining set is
filtered according to what the support of each family allows. Under a Gaussian
family the study used the fourteen equations listed in Section 4 other than
`ecxflat` (which was added to the set after the study was run), whereas under a
Beta and under a Gamma it used twelve.

```{r, echo = FALSE}
# One colour per convention, used consistently in every figure below. These were
# checked with a palette validator for lightness, chroma, contrast against the
# page and colour-vision separation rather than chosen by eye. Six hues are past
# the point where all-pairs separation is achievable at usable contrast: the
# measured/censored/deleted triple and the beta/gamma pair each separate under
# every simulated deficiency, and the one pair that does not -- censored and
# beta -- never shares a panel, because every figure facets by how the boundary
# is imposed.
arm_cols <- c(measured = "#1B5FAE", censored = "#C25E12", deleted = "#1F9E8C",
              floored = "#B0217A", beta = "#7C7A00", gamma = "#8E44C9")
arm_levels <- names(arm_cols)

# The facet variable in every figure. The distinction that matters is not
# four-versus-two but how the zero boundary comes to be imposed: not at all, by
# substituting or removing data, or implicitly as a side effect of choosing a
# response distribution.
arm_handling <- c(measured = "Measurement retained",
                  censored = "Measurement retained",
                  deleted = "Measurement retained",
                  floored = "Zero boundary imposed",
                  beta = "Zero bounded by the family",
                  gamma = "Zero bounded by the family")
handling_levels <- c("Measurement retained", "Zero boundary imposed",
                     "Zero bounded by the family")
```

## 5.2 Design

The data are generated from `nec4param`, which is in the candidate set of each
Gaussian convention, so that the generating shape is available to be recovered
and the weight it receives is itself informative.

```{r}
nec4param_curve <- function(x, top, bot, beta, nec) {
  bot + (top - bot) * exp(-exp(beta) * (x - nec) * (x >= nec))
}
```

The truth is parameterised so that the two experimental factors are explicit.
The control growth rate is set by the control fold-change `R` (the factor by
which the control population multiplies over a test of length `t`), and the
depth of the lower asymptote is set as a multiple `delta` of that control rate.
For example, at `delta = 2` the population declines twice as fast at the
highest concentrations as it grows in the control. The three levels used
(`delta` = 2, 4 and 8) span the range measured on the real datasets.

```{r}
sim_truth <- function(R = 2.3, delta = 4, t = 7, nec = 1.3, beta = -3.573) {
  mu_0 <- log(R) / t                    # true control growth rate
  bot <- -delta * mu_0                  # lower asymptote, delta x deeper than top
  list(R = R, delta = delta, t = t, top = mu_0, bot = bot,
       beta = beta, nec = nec,
       # where the true curve crosses zero growth
       zero_crossing = nec + log((1 + delta) / delta) / exp(beta))
}
```

The design is a geometric concentration series spanning a factor of one
hundred, with ten controls and five replicates at each of twelve concentrations
(n = 70), matching the real designs. The position of the highest concentration
is the second factor, and is expressed as a multiple `top_factor` of the
concentration at which the true curve crosses zero. At `top_factor = 1.0` the
series stops exactly at the crossing and about one design point in fourteen has
a non-positive true mean, whereas at `2.0` the series extends well past the
crossing and one design point in seven has a non-positive true mean. This
factor therefore determines how much negative data there is for a convention to
act on.

```{r}
sim_design <- function(truth, top_factor = 1, n_conc = 12, n_rep = 5,
                       n_control = 10, span = 100) {
  x_max <- top_factor * truth$zero_crossing
  concs <- exp(seq(log(x_max / span), log(x_max), length.out = n_conc))
  data.frame(x = c(rep(0, n_control), rep(concs, each = n_rep)))
}
```

The residual scale is constant along the curve, and the fits are homoscedastic
to match, so that no convention is misspecified in its variance and only the
mean structure is under test. When the within-dose standard deviation of the
`alga` data is measured against position on the curve (with substituted rows
excluded), only one of the four datasets shows a gradient at all
(`c_proliferum`, fitted ratio 14.1, slope p < 0.001), whereas the ratios for
the other three are 1.2 to 1.5 (p > 0.35). Generating all cells as
heteroscedastic would therefore assert as general a pattern seen in one
dataset. It would also misspecify each convention identically, which
is a confound shared by the whole comparison and not a feature of it.

```{r}
sim_sigma <- function(x, truth, sigma_0_abs) rep(sigma_0_abs, length(x))

simulate_dataset <- function(truth, design, sigma_0_abs, seed = NULL) {
  if (!is.null(seed)) set.seed(seed)
  mu <- nec4param_curve(design$x, truth$top, truth$bot, truth$beta, truth$nec)
  data.frame(x = design$x,
             y = rnorm(length(mu), mu, sim_sigma(design$x, truth, sigma_0_abs)))
}
```

The control residual standard deviation is held at a fixed absolute value,
anchored so that it corresponds to a coefficient of variation of 9.6% at `R` =
2.3, rather than at a fixed coefficient of variation. Under a fixed coefficient
of variation the generating model is exactly scale-equivariant in the growth
rate, so changing `R` would change nothing that is reported. Holding the
absolute error fixed instead means that increasing `R` increases the signal but
does not change the noise, which makes `R` an axis of measurement precision.

```{r}
sigma_0_abs <- 0.096 * log(2.3) / 7   # absolute control SD, anchored at R = 2.3
```

The design consists of seven cells. Four of them (`p1` to `p4`) have `delta` =
4 and `top_factor` = 2.0 and increasing values of `R`, giving four levels of
measurement precision with an otherwise identical design and truth. Two more
(`d2` and `d8`) change how far the true curve descends below zero, and the
seventh (`ctl`) stops the concentration series at the zero crossing, so that
the conventions have almost nothing to act on. Each cell was simulated 100
times and each simulated dataset was analysed under all six conventions, giving
7 x 100 x 6 = 4,200 model-averaged fits and 52,533 individual equation fits.
Sampling used four chains of 4,000 iterations with 2,000 warmup,
`adapt_delta = 0.99` and `max_treedepth = 12`. All fits are retained in the
summaries, as no unit failed and no estimate was unidentified.

```{r}
cells <- data.frame(
  cell = c("p1", "p2", "p3", "p4", "d2", "d8", "ctl"),
  delta = c(4, 4, 4, 4, 2, 8, 4),
  top_factor = c(2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 1),
  R = c(2.3, 3.3, 17, 73, 2.3, 2.3, 2.3)
)

cell_detail <- function(s) {
  tr <- sim_truth(R = s$R, delta = s$delta)
  dz <- sim_design(tr, top_factor = s$top_factor)
  mu <- nec4param_curve(dz$x, tr$top, tr$bot, tr$beta, tr$nec)
  true_ecx <- function(p) {
    target <- tr$top * (1 - p / 100)
    tr$nec + log((tr$top - tr$bot) / (target - tr$bot)) / exp(tr$beta)
  }
  data.frame(top = tr$top, bot = tr$bot, x_max = max(dz$x),
             ErC10 = true_ecx(10), ErC50 = true_ecx(50),
             cv_control = sigma_0_abs / tr$top,
             # tolerance, not `<= 0`: at top_factor = 1 the highest design point
             # sits exactly on the crossing, so a strict test flips on floating
             # point and reports 0 for some cells and 1/14 for others
             prop_negative = mean(mu <= 1e-10))
}
cells <- cbind(cells, do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(nrow(cells)),
                                            function(i) cell_detail(cells[i, ]))))
```

```{r, echo = FALSE}
knitr::kable(
  cells[, c("cell", "delta", "top_factor", "R", "top", "bot", "x_max",
            "ErC10", "ErC50", "cv_control", "prop_negative")],
  digits = c(0, 0, 1, 1, 3, 3, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3),
  col.names = c("Cell", "delta", "top factor", "R", "top", "bot", "max conc.",
                "true ErC10", "true ErC50", "control CV", "prop. negative"),
  caption = "**Table 1.** The seven simulated cells. `top` and `bot` are the true upper and lower asymptotes of the growth rate; `prop. negative` is the proportion of the 70 design points whose true mean growth rate is at or below zero. The true `nec` is 1.3 in every cell."
)
```

Three features of Table 1 are important for interpreting the results below.
First, the true ErC10 and ErC50 depend only on `delta`, so `p1` to `p4` share
one estimand and differ only in how precisely it can be measured. Second, the
proportion of negative true means is one in seven in all cells except `ctl`,
where it is one in fourteen, which in the realised datasets corresponds to
about 10 negative observations out of 70 (against about 2 in `ctl`). Third, the
control coefficient of variation falls from 9.6% to 1.9% across `p1` to `p4`,
and this is the precision sweep on which the rest of this section is built.

Figure 1 shows one simulated dataset from each cell. The scatter of the
observations relative to the range of the true curve narrows from `p1` to `p4`,
and in each cell except `ctl` the true curve is below zero growth at the
highest two concentrations, whereas in `ctl` the series ends where the true
curve crosses zero.

```{r cell-curves, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 5.5, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 1.** The seven simulated cells. Points are one simulated dataset per cell; the solid line is the true curve; the dashed horizontal line is zero growth. Vertical lines mark the true ErC10 (dotted) and ErC50 (dashed). Panels are labelled with the cell name, its design factors and the control coefficient of variation it realises."}
curve_data <- do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(nrow(cells)), function(i) {
  s <- cells[i, ]
  tr <- sim_truth(R = s$R, delta = s$delta)
  xx <- seq(0, s$x_max, length.out = 300)
  data.frame(cell = s$cell, x = xx,
             mu = nec4param_curve(xx, tr$top, tr$bot, tr$beta, tr$nec))
}))

point_data <- do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(nrow(cells)), function(i) {
  s <- cells[i, ]
  tr <- sim_truth(R = s$R, delta = s$delta)
  d <- simulate_dataset(tr, sim_design(tr, s$top_factor), sigma_0_abs,
                        seed = 42 + i)
  data.frame(cell = s$cell, d)
}))

# Panels in design order -- the precision sweep first, then the cells that change
# the truth or the design -- rather than alphabetically.
cell_levels <- c("p1", "p2", "p3", "p4", "d2", "d8", "ctl")
cells$cell <- factor(cells$cell, levels = cell_levels)
curve_data$cell <- factor(curve_data$cell, levels = cell_levels)
point_data$cell <- factor(point_data$cell, levels = cell_levels)

lab <- setNames(
  sprintf("%s:  delta %g,  top %.1f,  R %.1f\n(control CV %.1f%%)",
          cells$cell, cells$delta, cells$top_factor, cells$R,
          100 * cells$cv_control),
  levels(cells$cell))

# controls sit at x = 0 and have no position on a log axis, so they are dropped
# from this figure; every fit uses them
ggplot(subset(point_data, x > 0), aes(x = x, y = y)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_vline(data = cells, aes(xintercept = ErC10),
             linetype = "dotted", colour = "steelblue") +
  geom_vline(data = cells, aes(xintercept = ErC50),
             linetype = "dashed", colour = "steelblue") +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.45, size = 0.8) +
  geom_line(data = subset(curve_data, x > 0), aes(y = mu), linewidth = 0.7) +
  facet_wrap(~ cell, scales = "free", ncol = 4,
             labeller = labeller(cell = lab)) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  labs(x = "Concentration (log scale)",
       y = expression("Specific growth rate ("*d^-1*")")) +
  theme_classic(base_size = 8) +
  theme(panel.spacing = unit(0.35, "lines"),
        plot.margin = margin(2, 4, 2, 2),
        strip.background = element_blank(), strip.text = element_text(hjust = 0))
```

