panel_fit$arm <- factor(panel_fit$arm, levels = arm_levels)

# Each panel shows the data its own convention was given, not one intact copy
# repeated six times: `floored`, `beta` and `gamma` were handed zeros in place of
# the negative values, `censored` the same zeros declared as upper bounds, and
# `deleted` those rows not at all. Open symbols mark the rows the convention
# altered or dropped, drawn where that convention put them -- at zero for the
# four that substitute, and at the measured value for `deleted`, which is what
# shows the reader what was discarded.
n_neg <- sum(panel_dat$sgr < 0)
substituting <- c("floored", "beta", "gamma", "censored")
panel_points <- do.call(rbind, lapply(arm_levels, function(a) {
  d <- panel_dat
  # `measured` alters nothing, so nothing in its panel is marked.
  d$altered <- d$sgr < 0 & a != "measured"
  d$y <- if (a %in% substituting) pmax(d$sgr, 0) else d$sgr
  data.frame(arm = a, x = d$x, y = d$y, altered = d$altered)
}))
panel_points$arm <- factor(panel_points$arm, levels = arm_levels)

# `cells` records the true asymptotes and the true ErC50 but not `beta` or
# `nec`, so the curve is rebuilt from the same sim_truth() call the cell was
# generated by rather than from the table.
panel_cell <- cells[cells$cell == "p1", ]
panel_truth <- sim_truth(R = panel_cell$R, delta = panel_cell$delta)
panel_true <- data.frame(
  x = exp(seq(log(min(panel_fit$x)), log(max(panel_fit$x)), length.out = 200)))
panel_true$y <- nec4param_curve(panel_true$x, panel_truth$top, panel_truth$bot,
                                panel_truth$beta, panel_truth$nec)
```


The tables and figures below summarise each cell over its 100 realisations, and
such a summary does not show what a convention does to an individual dataset.
We therefore also fitted all six conventions to a single simulated dataset from
`p1`, the noisiest cell tested, in which
`r sprintf("%.0f", 70 * cells$prop_negative[cells$cell == "p1"])` of the seventy
design points have a true mean growth rate at or below zero and
`r sum(panel_dat$sgr < 0)` of the seventy simulated observations were negative
(Figure 2). This realisation is the one drawn for the `p1` exemplar by a seed
fixed on the cell and convention names, so it was chosen before any result was
seen and not selected afterwards for the picture it makes.


```{r one-dataset, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 5.4, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = sprintf("**Figure 2.** One simulated dataset from cell `p1`, fitted under each of the six conventions. Points are the data each convention was given; open symbols mark the %d observations it altered or dropped, drawn at zero by the four conventions that substitute and at their measured values under `deleted`, which discards those rows. `measured` alters none, so nothing in its panel is marked. The coloured line is the model-averaged posterior median and the band its 95%% credible interval; the black line is the true curve. The dashed horizontal line is zero growth and the dashed vertical line the true ErC50. The controls sit at a concentration of zero and have no position on a log axis, so they are not drawn; every fit uses them.", n_neg)}
ggplot(panel_fit, aes(x = x)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey60") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = panel_cell$ErC50, linetype = "dashed",
             colour = "grey60") +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lo, ymax = hi, fill = arm), alpha = 0.25) +
  geom_point(data = subset(panel_points, x > 0),
             aes(y = y, shape = altered), size = 1.1, alpha = 0.7) +
  geom_line(data = panel_true, aes(y = y), linewidth = 1, colour = "grey10") +
  geom_line(aes(y = y, colour = arm), linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c(`FALSE` = 16, `TRUE` = 1), guide = "none") +
  scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols, guide = "none") +
  scale_fill_manual(values = arm_cols, guide = "none") +
  scale_x_log10() +
  facet_wrap(~ arm, ncol = 3) +
  labs(x = "Concentration (log scale)",
       y = expression("Specific growth rate ("*d^-1*")")) +
  theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
  theme(strip.background = element_blank(),
        strip.text = element_text(hjust = 0))
```


Under `measured` the model-averaged curve follows the true curve along its
whole length, including the part below zero, and its credible band is narrow
throughout (Figure 2). Under `censored` the median also follows the true curve,
although the band widens once the curve passes zero, because a row declared to
be below a bound provides less information about where the curve goes than a
measurement does.

In contrast, the three conventions that put a zero in place of the measurement
(`floored`, `beta` and `gamma`) produce a curve that flattens onto zero instead
of continuing to decline. This flattening appears from a little below the true
ErC50 (the dashed vertical line), and therefore begins at lower concentrations
than the substituted rows themselves. This is likely to be the mechanism behind
the biases reported below: the substitution is made at the top of the
concentration series, but the resulting curve is displaced over a range that
includes the concentrations from which the estimates are read. The displacement
is small at the ErC50 and large at the low-concentration end of the curve, where
the NSEC is read, and this ordering is measured in Sections 5.4 and 5.6.

The `deleted` panel shows a different consequence. In this dataset, removing
all rows with a negative value removed the highest concentrations entirely, so
the fitted curve stops where the surviving data stop, and the design is no
longer the one that was run.

The `gamma` panel shows a failure of the variance and not of the mean. Its
credible band is widest at the control (on the left of the panel), where the
generated residual spread is no wider than at any other concentration, because
an identity-link Gamma ties the spread to the fitted mean. We quantify this
with a posterior predictive check in Section 5.4.

## 5.4 ErC10 and ErC50

Relative bias is the mean of the estimated value minus the true value over the
100 realisations of a cell, expressed as a percentage of the true value. The
precision sweep is the most informative comparison in the study, because it
distinguishes the two sources of error in an estimate: estimation error shrinks
as an experiment becomes more precise, whereas the error due to model
misspecification does not.

```{r precision-bias, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 4.6, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 3.** Relative bias in the estimated ErC10 and ErC50 against the control coefficient of variation, which decreases to the right as the experiment becomes more precise. Each point summarises 100 simulated datasets. The dashed line is zero bias."}
bias_plot <- function(d, ests) {
  ggplot(subset(d, estimate %in% ests),
         aes(x = cv_f, y = rel_bias, colour = arm, group = arm)) +
    geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
    geom_line(linewidth = 0.7) +
    geom_point(size = 1.8) +
    # One label per series at the right-hand edge rather than a key: the label
    # sits on the line it names, which is the secondary encoding the palette
    # note above depends on.
    scale_x_discrete(expand = expansion(add = c(0.4, 0.4))) +
    facet_grid(estimate ~ handling, scales = "free_y") +
    labs(x = "Control coefficient of variation (%)  —  more precise →",
         y = "Relative bias in the estimate (%)") +
    scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols, breaks = arm_levels) +
    guides(colour = guide_legend(nrow = 1)) +
    theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
    theme(strip.background = element_blank(), legend.position = "bottom",
          legend.title = element_blank(), legend.key.width = unit(1.4, "lines"))
}
bias_plot(precision, c("ErC10", "ErC50"))
```

The three conventions that retain the measurement converge on the true ErC50
(Figure 3). Across `p1` to `p4` the bias of `measured` ranges from
`r f1(val("measured", "ErC50", "p1"))`% to
`r f1(val("measured", "ErC50", "p4"))`%, that of `censored` from
`r f1(val("censored", "ErC50", "p1"))`% to
`r f1(val("censored", "ErC50", "p4"))`%, and that of `deleted` from
`r f1(val("deleted", "ErC50", "p1"))`% to
`r f1(val("deleted", "ErC50", "p4"))`%, so that all three are within half a per
cent of the truth at the highest precision tested.

In contrast, flooring does not converge. The magnitude of the ErC50 bias of
`floored` falls from `r f1(val("floored", "ErC50", "p1"))`% to
`r f1(val("floored", "ErC50", "p4"))`% and then stops falling, and that of
`beta` remains between `r f1(val("beta", "ErC50", "p1"))`% and
`r f1(val("beta", "ErC50", "p4"))`% throughout, so both conventions make the
substance appear more toxic than it is. This residual bias is smaller than the 8
to 15% reported for these conventions under a fixed `nec4param`, because the
candidate set contains the zero-asymptote shapes that floored data resemble
(Section 4), and averaging over these shapes absorbs part of the distortion.
However, it does not absorb all of it.

The Gamma converges on the true ErC50, but is the least accurate of the six
conventions on ErC10. Its ErC50 bias falls from
`r f1(val("gamma", "ErC50", "p1"))`% to
`r f1(val("gamma", "ErC50", "p4"))`% across the sweep, whereas its ErC10 is
displaced by `r f1(val("gamma", "ErC10", "p1"))`% at the noisiest and
`r f1(val("gamma", "ErC10", "p4"))`% at the most precise. This bias increases
as the experiment becomes more precise, which indicates misspecification and
not estimation error.

The cause is in the variance and not in the mean, as a posterior predictive
check shows. These data are generated with a residual standard deviation that is
constant along the curve, so what a correctly specified family should predict is
known exactly (i.e. the generated value at each concentration). Each fit of the
four precision cells was refitted, and its model-averaged predicted spread was
compared with the generated one, for 100 realisations per cell and convention.
We summarise the comparison by the control ratio (the predicted spread at the
lowest concentration divided by the generated value) and the gradient (the ratio
of the predicted spread at the control to that at the highest concentration),
both of which should therefore be one for a correctly specified family.

```{r, echo = FALSE}
# Predicted residual spread against the generated one, transcribed from
# results/ppc_spread.csv in the compendium at the commit named in Section 9.
# `control_ratio` is the predicted spread at the lowest concentration divided by
# the generated value, averaged over the realisations of that cell, with the
# interval covering 95% of them; `gradient_median` is the median ratio of the
# predicted spread at the lowest concentration to that at the highest.
ppc <- read.csv(text = "
cell,arm,n,control_ratio,control_ratio_lo,control_ratio_hi,gradient_median
p1,measured,100,1,0.86,1.18,1.03
p2,measured,100,0.98,0.84,1.16,1.03
p3,measured,100,1,0.86,1.16,1.03
p4,measured,100,0.98,0.81,1.15,1.03
p1,floored,100,0.91,0.77,1.07,1.04
p2,floored,100,0.93,0.78,1.15,1.04
p3,floored,100,1.02,0.84,1.19,1.05
p4,floored,100,1.12,0.98,1.31,1.05
p1,gamma,100,2.51,1.25,4.21,180.62
p2,gamma,100,2.52,1.36,4.96,154.36
p3,gamma,100,2.45,1.64,3.93,105.01
p4,gamma,100,2.69,2,4.7,90.06
p1,beta,100,1.09,0.86,1.37,2.66
p2,beta,100,1.09,0.84,1.44,2.56
p3,beta,100,1.08,0.82,1.37,1.96
p4,beta,100,1.1,0.85,1.37,1.71
")
ppc$arm <- factor(ppc$arm, levels = arm_levels)
sv <- function(a, cl, what = "control_ratio") {
  ppc[[what]][ppc$arm == a & ppc$cell == cl]
}
```

Because `measured` is correctly specified by construction, its values are the
closest this statistic comes to one on these data, and provide the reference
against which the other conventions are read: a control ratio of
`r f2(sv("measured", "p1"))` to `r f2(sv("measured", "p4"))`, with 95% of
realisations between `r f2(min(ppc$control_ratio_lo[ppc$arm == "measured"]))`
and `r f2(max(ppc$control_ratio_hi[ppc$arm == "measured"]))`, and a
gradient of `r f2(sv("measured", "p1", "gradient_median"))`.

An identity-link Gamma cannot reproduce a constant spread, because it ties the
residual standard deviation to the mean. It overstates the spread at the
control by a factor of `r f2(sv("gamma", "p1"))` to `r f2(sv("gamma", "p4"))`,
and its gradient ranges from `r sprintf("%.0f", sv("gamma", "p1", "gradient_median"))`
at the noisiest cell to `r sprintf("%.0f", sv("gamma", "p4", "gradient_median"))`
at the most precise, against a true value of one. The floored block of
observations is therefore given almost all of the weight in the likelihood and
the control scatter almost none, so that it is the fit at the shoulder of the
curve (where a 10% decline is read, i.e. the ErC10) that is distorted.

This check also shows what flooring does and does not damage. Under a
Gaussian family the floored data are described by a correctly specified
variance model, with a control ratio for `floored` of
`r f2(sv("floored", "p1"))` to `r f2(sv("floored", "p4"))` and a gradient of
`r f2(sv("floored", "p1", "gradient_median"))`, both within the reference. What the
substitution changes is the mean structure (examined in Section 5.7), whereas
under a Gamma the failures of the mean and of the variance compound.

The Beta is a third case. Its control ratio of `r f2(sv("beta", "p1"))` to
`r f2(sv("beta", "p4"))` is within the reference, and its gradient of
`r f2(sv("beta", "p4", "gradient_median"))` to `r f2(sv("beta", "p1", "gradient_median"))`
is a mild departure compared with that of the Gamma. Whatever displaces the
ErC10 of the Beta is therefore not the variance model.

At the highest precision tested, the intervals of `gamma` are
`r f2(val("gamma", "ErC50", "p4", "width") / val("measured", "ErC50", "p4", "width"))`
times as wide as those of `measured` on ErC50, which keeps its ErC50 coverage
nominal while its point estimate for ErC10 is a quarter low.

The Beta is displaced in the other direction on ErC10, from
`r f1(val("beta", "ErC10", "p1"))`% at the noisiest to
`r f1(val("beta", "ErC10", "p4"))`% at the most precise. This bias also
increases as the experiment becomes more precise, which again indicates
misspecification and not estimation error.

```{r design-bias, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 5, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 4.** Relative bias in the three cells that change the truth or the design rather than the measurement precision, all at a control coefficient of variation of 9.6%. `d2` and `d8` change how far the true curve descends below zero; `ctl` stops the concentration series at the zero crossing, leaving about two negative observations in seventy for a convention to act on."}
ggplot(design_cells, aes(x = rel_bias, y = arm, colour = arm)) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_point(size = 2.2) +
  # estimate on the columns, not the rows: facet_grid frees a scale per column,
  # and the three estimates are on scales that differ by an order of magnitude,
  # so sharing one x axis across them would hide everything but the NSEC.
  facet_grid(cell ~ estimate, scales = "free_x") +
  scale_y_discrete(limits = rev(arm_levels)) +
  labs(x = "Relative bias in the estimate (%)", y = NULL) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols, guide = "none") +
  theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
  theme(strip.background = element_blank(), legend.position = "none")
```

The depth to which the curve descends below zero changes the size of the
effect, but not its direction (Figure 4). Between `d2` and `d8` the ErC50 bias
of `floored` is `r f1(val("floored", "ErC50", "d2"))`% and
`r f1(val("floored", "ErC50", "d8"))`%, and that of `beta`
`r f1(val("beta", "ErC50", "d2"))`% and
`r f1(val("beta", "ErC50", "d8"))`%, against
`r f1(val("measured", "ErC50", "d2"))`% and
`r f1(val("measured", "ErC50", "d8"))`% for `measured`.

The `ctl` cell is the one that indicates whether any of this applies to a
reader's own data.
Its concentration series stops at the zero crossing, so a realised dataset
contains about two negative observations instead of ten, and the four Gaussian
conventions then span `r f1(spread("ErC50", "ctl"))` percentage points of ErC50
bias between them. This compares with `r f1(spread("ErC50", "p1"))` in `p1`,
which has equal control variability and four times as many negative
observations. Where essentially none of the observations are at or below zero
growth, the choice of convention is therefore unlikely to change the reported
value.

## 5.5 Credible interval coverage

Coverage is the proportion of the 100 realisations whose 95% credible interval
contains the true value. We report it together with the mean interval width,
because a wide interval and an accurate one both achieve nominal coverage,
although they are different results.

```{r precision-coverage, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 4.6, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 5.** Proportion of 95% credible intervals containing the true ErC10 or ErC50, against the control coefficient of variation. The dashed line is nominal coverage."}
cov_plot <- function(d, ests) {
  ggplot(subset(d, estimate %in% ests),
         aes(x = cv_f, y = coverage, colour = arm, group = arm)) +
    geom_hline(yintercept = 0.95, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
    geom_line(linewidth = 0.7) +
    geom_point(size = 1.8) +
    scale_x_discrete(expand = expansion(add = c(0.4, 0.4))) +
    scale_y_continuous(limits = c(0, 1)) +
    facet_grid(estimate ~ handling) +
    labs(x = "Control coefficient of variation (%)  —  more precise →",
         y = "Proportion of 95% intervals containing the truth") +
    scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols, breaks = arm_levels) +
    guides(colour = guide_legend(nrow = 1)) +
    theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
    theme(strip.background = element_blank(), legend.position = "bottom",
          legend.title = element_blank(), legend.key.width = unit(1.4, "lines"))
}
cov_plot(precision, c("ErC10", "ErC50"))
```

The three conventions that retain the measurement maintain nominal coverage
across the sweep (Figure 5). At the highest precision the interval of
`measured` contains the true ErC50 in
`r f2(val("measured", "ErC50", "p4", "coverage"))` of the 100 realisations,
compared with `r f2(val("censored", "ErC50", "p4", "coverage"))` for `censored`
and `r f2(val("deleted", "ErC50", "p4", "coverage"))` for `deleted`.

In contrast, the coverage of `floored` falls as the experiment becomes more
precise, from `r f2(val("floored", "ErC50", "p1", "coverage"))` to
`r f2(val("floored", "ErC50", "p4", "coverage"))` on ErC50, and that of `beta`
from `r f2(val("beta", "ErC50", "p1", "coverage"))` to
`r f2(val("beta", "ErC50", "p4", "coverage"))`. This is the practical
consequence of a bias that does not converge, because the interval narrows
around a centre that remains displaced. A laboratory that improves its
technique, adds replicates and reduces its control variability is therefore
likely, if it floors its negative values, to produce an analysis that is more
confident and no less wrong. The ErC10 of `gamma` shows this failure more
severely,
with coverage falling from `r f2(val("gamma", "ErC10", "p1", "coverage"))` to
`r f2(val("gamma", "ErC10", "p4", "coverage"))`.

Without the interval width, these coverage results could be read the wrong way
round. At the highest precision `floored` has an ErC50 interval
`r f2(val("floored", "ErC50", "p4", "width") / val("measured", "ErC50", "p4", "width"))`
times as wide as that of `measured` and contains the truth in a quarter of the
realisations, whereas `gamma` has an interval
`r f2(val("gamma", "ErC50", "p4", "width") / val("measured", "ErC50", "p4", "width"))`
times as wide and contains the truth at the nominal rate. Neither coverage nor
width should therefore be reported without the other.

## 5.6 The NSEC

ErC10 and the NSEC are both commonly used as a no-effect value, but the
conventions affect them differently. Because the true `nec`
is the limiting value of the NSEC as the residual error goes to zero, and not
its expected value at any finite level of noise (Section 2.3), an approach is
identified by the precision sweep and not by any single cell. A correctly
specified approach remains on the truth as the experiment becomes more precise,
whereas a misspecified one departs further from it the more precise the
experiment becomes.

```{r nsec-precision, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 5.4, out.width = "100%", fig.cap = "**Figure 6.** Relative bias in the estimated NSEC against the true `nec` of 1.3 (upper row) and the proportion of 95% credible intervals containing it (lower row), against the control coefficient of variation. The dashed lines are zero bias and nominal coverage. A correctly specified approach stays on zero bias as the residual error falls; a misspecified one departs further the more precise the experiment becomes."}
# Bias and coverage in one figure, as two rows of a facet rather than two plots
# stacked by a layout package: the reference line differs between the rows, so
# it is supplied as data rather than as a constant.
nsec_long <- subset(precision, estimate == "NSEC") |>
  select(arm, handling, cv_f, `Relative bias (%)` = rel_bias,
         `Interval coverage` = coverage) |>
  pivot_longer(c(`Relative bias (%)`, `Interval coverage`),
               names_to = "metric", values_to = "value") |>
  mutate(metric = factor(metric, levels = c("Relative bias (%)",
                                            "Interval coverage")))
nsec_ref <- data.frame(metric = factor(c("Relative bias (%)",
                                         "Interval coverage"),
                                       levels = levels(nsec_long$metric)),
                       yint = c(0, 0.95))

ggplot(nsec_long, aes(x = cv_f, y = value, colour = arm, group = arm)) +
  geom_hline(data = nsec_ref, aes(yintercept = yint),
             linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.7) +
  geom_point(size = 1.8) +
  scale_x_discrete(expand = expansion(add = c(0.4, 0.4))) +
  facet_grid(metric ~ handling, scales = "free_y", switch = "y") +
  labs(x = "Control coefficient of variation (%)  —  more precise \u2192",
       y = NULL) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = arm_cols, breaks = arm_levels) +
  guides(colour = guide_legend(nrow = 1)) +
  theme_classic(base_size = 9) +
  theme(strip.background = element_blank(), strip.placement = "outside",
        legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank(),
        legend.key.width = unit(1.4, "lines"))
```

The retaining conventions remain near the true `nec` across the sweep, with no
trend in either direction (Figure 6). At the highest precision the NSEC bias of
`measured` is `r f1(val("measured", "NSEC", "p4"))`%, that of `censored`
`r f1(val("censored", "NSEC", "p4"))`% and that of `deleted`
`r f1(val("deleted", "NSEC", "p4"))`%. These biases are not constant across
the sweep (the largest single departure of the three is
`r f1(max(abs(sapply(c("p1","p2","p3","p4"), function(cl) sapply(c("measured","censored","deleted"), val, "NSEC", cl)))))`%,
for `measured` at a control coefficient of variation of 6.7%), but the
departures do not grow as the experiment becomes more precise, and this is what
distinguishes estimation error from misspecification.

Section 2.3 gives the reason to expect an overestimate at finite noise. Under
the priors used here, however, this overshoot is small enough to be lost in the
Monte Carlo error of 100 realisations, and does not appear as a trend. It was
visible under the superseded prior, where these three conventions ranged from
about +14% down to +4%. A correctly specified approach is therefore identified
here by remaining on the truth as the residual error falls, and not by the
direction from which it approaches.

In contrast, the conventions that replace the measurement diverge downward. The
NSEC bias of `floored` ranges from `r f1(val("floored", "NSEC", "p1"))`% to
`r f1(val("floored", "NSEC", "p4"))`%, that of `beta` from
`r f1(val("beta", "NSEC", "p1"))`% to `r f1(val("beta", "NSEC", "p4"))`%, and
that of `gamma` from `r f1(val("gamma", "NSEC", "p1"))`% to
`r f1(val("gamma", "NSEC", "p4"))`%. Coverage follows this pattern: at the
highest precision the interval of `floored` contains the true `nec` in
`r f2(val("floored", "NSEC", "p4", "coverage"))` of the 100 realisations and
that of `gamma` in `r f2(val("gamma", "NSEC", "p4", "coverage"))`, against
`r f2(val("censored", "NSEC", "p4", "coverage"))` for `censored` and
`r f2(val("deleted", "NSEC", "p4", "coverage"))` for `deleted`.

For a convention that floors, the errors in ErC50 and in the NSEC are in the
same direction. Such a convention returns an ErC50 and an NSEC that are both too
low, so the substance appears more toxic than it is on both estimates, which for
a guideline value is the cautious direction in which to be wrong. However, being
cautious is different from being correct. At the highest precision tested the
NSEC of `floored` is displaced by more than half the true value, which is an
error comparable in size to the estimate, and its interval contains the truth in
none of the 100 realisations. An estimate that provides no information about
the quantity it names cannot be relied on to be cautious on the next dataset
either.

`measured` is the only convention whose NSEC coverage falls without its bias
diverging, from `r f2(val("measured", "NSEC", "p1", "coverage"))` to
`r f2(val("measured", "NSEC", "p4", "coverage"))`. This is the property of the
metric described in Section 2.3, and not a failure of the convention. The NSEC
overshoots the true `nec` slightly at finite noise, and by `p4` the interval has
narrowed faster than the remaining bias of
`r f1(val("measured", "NSEC", "p4"))`% has closed, so that an interval
correctly placed to within a few per cent no longer contains the truth. Over
this range `censored` and `deleted` maintain nominal coverage, with intervals
`r f2(val("censored", "NSEC", "p4", "width") / val("measured", "NSEC", "p4", "width"))`
and
`r f2(val("deleted", "NSEC", "p4", "width") / val("measured", "NSEC", "p4", "width"))`
times as wide.

## 5.7 The equations the data are made to resemble

Each convention is model-averaged over the declining set for its family, and
the data were generated from `nec4param`, so the weight each convention gives
to `nec4param` measures how closely the data, once the convention has been
applied, still resemble the process that produced them. This weight describes
the data supplied to the fit and not the substance, and we report it because it
explains the biases above.

```{r, echo = FALSE}
# Mean model weight per cell and arm, transcribed from the compendium at the
# commit named in Section 9. `w_nec4param` is the weight on the generating
# equation; `top_model` is the equation with the highest mean weight in that
# cell and arm.
weights <- read.csv(text = "
cell,arm,w_nec4param,w_zero_asymptote,n_models,top_model,top_weight
p1,measured,0.506,0,12.9,nec4param,0.533
p1,censored,0.3,0.006,12.9,neclin,0.392
p1,deleted,0.111,0.432,13,neclin,0.275
p1,floored,0.066,0.631,12.9,ecxsigm,0.283
p1,beta,0.066,0.078,10.8,ecx4param,0.391
p1,gamma,0.036,0.033,11.1,ecxwb1,0.756
p2,measured,0.714,0,12.9,nec4param,0.717
p2,censored,0.355,0.003,13,neclin,0.506
p2,deleted,0.191,0.31,13.2,neclin,0.382
p2,floored,0.061,0.658,13,ecxsigm,0.362
p2,beta,0.05,0.064,10.8,ecxwb2,0.43
p2,gamma,0.021,0.013,11.1,ecxwb1,0.803
p3,measured,0.849,0,12.9,nec4param,0.865
p3,censored,0.344,0.001,13.1,neclin,0.599
p3,deleted,0.254,0.135,13.4,neclin,0.608
p3,floored,0.003,0.891,13.1,ecxsigm,0.805
p3,beta,0.017,0.035,10.8,ecxwb2,0.631
p3,gamma,0,0,11.2,ecxwb1,0.891
p4,measured,0.852,0,13,nec4param,0.861
p4,censored,0.469,0,13,nec4param,0.62
p4,deleted,0.363,0.031,13.4,neclin,0.646
p4,floored,0,0.978,13,ecxsigm,0.962
p4,beta,0.01,0.041,10.8,ecxwb2,0.822
p4,gamma,0,0,11.2,ecxwb1,0.915
d2,measured,0.455,0,13,nec4param,0.467
d2,censored,0.271,0.006,12.9,neclin,0.355
d2,deleted,0.127,0.391,13.1,neclin,0.263
d2,floored,0.071,0.636,12.9,ecxsigm,0.287
d2,beta,0.06,0.133,10.8,ecx4param,0.36
d2,gamma,0.036,0.037,11,ecxwb1,0.781
d8,measured,0.376,0,13,neclin,0.609
d8,censored,0.319,0.003,13.1,neclin,0.418
d8,deleted,0.138,0.407,13.1,neclin,0.28
d8,floored,0.095,0.62,13.1,ecxsigm,0.27
d8,beta,0.101,0.066,10.9,ecxwb2,0.401
d8,gamma,0.037,0.025,11.1,ecxwb1,0.736
ctl,measured,0.162,0.275,12.9,neclin,0.375
ctl,censored,0.163,0.288,13,neclin,0.367
ctl,deleted,0.169,0.443,12.9,neclin,0.293
ctl,floored,0.193,0.33,13,neclin,0.34
ctl,beta,0.014,0.795,10.9,ecxwb1p3,0.366
ctl,gamma,0.033,0.716,11.1,ecxwb1p3,0.292
")
```

```{r, echo = FALSE}
knitr::kable(
  weights |>
    filter(cell %in% c("p1", "p2", "p3", "p4")) |>
    mutate(arm = factor(arm, levels = arm_levels)) |>
    select(cell, arm, w_nec4param) |>
    pivot_wider(names_from = cell, values_from = w_nec4param) |>
    arrange(arm),
  col.names = c("Convention", "p1 (CV 9.6%)", "p2 (CV 6.7%)",
                "p3 (CV 2.8%)", "p4 (CV 1.9%)"),
  digits = 3,
  caption = "**Table 2.** Mean model weight on the generating equation `nec4param` across the precision sweep, averaged over 100 realisations per cell. The remaining weight is spread over the other eleven to thirteen equations in the candidate set."
)
```

Given the measurements as recorded, model averaging identifies the generating
equation, and does so more clearly as the experiment becomes more precise
(Table 2): `nec4param` receives
`r f2(weights$w_nec4param[weights$cell == "p1" & weights$arm == "measured"])`
of the weight at a control coefficient of variation of 9.6% and
`r f2(weights$w_nec4param[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "measured"])`
at 1.9%. Under `censored` and `deleted` it still receives
`r f2(weights$w_nec4param[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "censored"])`
and `r f2(weights$w_nec4param[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "deleted"])`
of the weight at the highest precision, with most of the remainder given to
`neclin`, which shares its breakpoint and differs only in the shape below it.

In contrast, under the conventions that replace the measurement the generating
equation receives essentially none of the weight, which goes instead to a
zero-asymptote shape. At the highest precision `floored` gives `nec4param`
`r f2(weights$w_nec4param[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "floored"])`
of the weight and `ecxsigm`
`r f2(weights$top_weight[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "floored"])`,
whereas `gamma` gives `ecxwb1`
`r f2(weights$top_weight[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "gamma"])`.
Both of these equations decay smoothly to zero, which is what floored data look
like, and the weights favour them more strongly the more precise the
experiment. A model weight is evidence about which shape fits the data
supplied, and not about which process produced the measurements, and on floored
data these are different questions.

The six equations whose lower asymptote is fixed at zero provide the clearest
signature of the substitution, although they identify it only where the
substitution itself imposes the boundary. At the highest precision these
equations receive
`r f2(weights$w_zero_asymptote[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "floored"])`
of the weight under `floored`, against
`r f2(weights$w_zero_asymptote[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "measured"])`
under `measured` and
`r f2(weights$w_zero_asymptote[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "censored"])`
under `censored`. Under `beta` and `gamma` they receive only
`r f2(weights$w_zero_asymptote[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "beta"])`
and
`r f2(weights$w_zero_asymptote[weights$cell == "p4" & weights$arm == "gamma"])`,
because these families already exclude a negative mean, and an equation with a
free lower asymptote is bounded below in any case. The weight on these six
equations is therefore a diagnostic for flooring under an unbounded family, and
not for the zero boundary in general.

# 6 Worked case studies

The simulation establishes which conventions recover the truth, but it cannot
show how large the difference is on a real dataset, where the truth is unknown
and the design, the noise and the shape of the response are not under the
analyst's control. We therefore analysed four marine microalgal growth tests
under all six conventions, and compared each estimate with the `measured`
estimate on that dataset.

The four tests are included in `bayesnec` as the `alga` data. Two are tests on
*Cladocopium proliferum* (a coral endosymbiont) over seven days, and two are on
*Rhodomonas salina* over three days, with each species exposed to two
contaminants on undisclosed and mutually incomparable concentration scales. The
summary of the datasets in Table 3 is computed from the data that ship with the
package, so it describes whichever version you have installed.

```{r}
data(alga)

# The four case studies are the four species-by-contaminant cells of `alga`.
# The names used throughout this section are the vignette's; the mapping is by
# cell rather than by row order, so it survives any reordering of the data.
alga$dataset <- factor(
  paste0(as.character(alga$species), ifelse(alga$contaminant == "B", "2", "")),
  levels = c("c_proliferum", "c_proliferum2", "r_salina", "r_salina2"))

# The growth rate implied by the counting limit of 10 cells per mL, against the
# starting density recorded for the test. Where the source substituted a zero
# for a below-limit count -- flagged `sgr_source == "substituted"` -- this is the
# rate the substitution implies, and it is what the retaining conventions use in
# place of the substituted zero.
alga$below_limit <- alga$sgr_source == "substituted"
alga$limit_rate <- (log(10) - log(alga$density_initial)) / alga$days
alga$y <- ifelse(alga$below_limit, alga$limit_rate, alga$sgr)

datasets <- alga |>
  group_by(dataset, species, duration_d = days) |>
  summarise(n = n(), n_conc = n_distinct(dose),
            control_rate = mean(y[dose == 0]),
            below_limit = sum(below_limit),
            n_negative = sum(y < 0),
            prop_at_or_below_zero = mean(y <= 0), .groups = "drop") |>
  mutate(fold_change_R = exp(control_rate * duration_d), .before = below_limit)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE}
# Transposed: nine columns against four rows reads badly, and the datasets are
# the natural columns because every later figure compares across them. Each
# value is formatted before transposing -- t() coerces to character, so kable's
# `digits` no longer applies once the table is on its side.
datasets_t <- with(datasets, data.frame(
  Species = as.character(species),
  Days = as.character(duration_d),
  n = as.character(n),
  Concentrations = as.character(n_conc),
  `Control rate (per d)` = sprintf("%.3f", control_rate),
  `Fold change R` = sprintf("%.1f", fold_change_R),
  `Below limit` = as.character(below_limit),
  `Negative` = as.character(n_negative),
  `Prop. at or below zero` = sprintf("%.2f", prop_at_or_below_zero),
  check.names = FALSE, stringsAsFactors = FALSE))
datasets_t <- as.data.frame(t(datasets_t), stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
names(datasets_t) <- datasets$dataset
knitr::kable(datasets_t,
  caption = "**Table 3.** The four case-study datasets, computed from the `alga` data that ship with the package. `Below limit` counts the rows whose population fell below the counting limit, for which the source substituted a zero. All four have a substantial proportion of observations at or below zero growth, so all four are in the regime where Section 5 applies.")
```

## 6.1 Undefined growth rates in the *Rhodomonas* tests

At the highest concentrations in both *Rhodomonas* tests the population fell
below the counting limit of 10 cells per mL, so the density is recorded as zero
and the specific growth rate is *undefined*, not negative. These rows are
flagged `sgr_source == "substituted"` in `alga` (16 on `r_salina` and 4 on
`r_salina2`, Table 3). The *Cladocopium* tests have no such rows, and their
negative values are measurements.

Because there is no measured value to retain in these rows, each convention
must substitute something. The `measured` convention uses the growth rate
implied by the counting limit, which is itself a substitution, so on these two
datasets it is not a true analysis of the measurements, and the comparison is
correspondingly weaker. This situation is likely to be common with real algal
data, and is one of the reasons left-censoring is useful, as it can express "at or below the
limit" without inventing a value.

The undefined rows also determine what `censored` means on these two datasets.
Left-censoring declares that the true value is at or below a bound, so the bound
should be the tightest one the measurement supports. This is zero for a growth
rate that was measured and was negative, but the counting limit itself for a row
that fell below it. The latter is a stronger statement than "below zero", and is
the one thing the test does establish about that row.

## 6.2 Estimates from each convention

Each dataset is prepared by each convention exactly as in Section 5.1, and
passed to `bnec()` with package defaults, so the conventions and candidate sets
in the case studies match those in the simulation.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
# Every convention takes bnec()'s own default priors here, unlike the
# simulation, where one prior is fixed per cell and convention so that repeated
# realisations compile one Stan program rather than a hundred. Each dataset is
# fitted once, so there is nothing for a fixed prior to save, and taking the
# defaults is the practice under examination.
fit_case <- function(d, convention) {
  d$y <- ifelse(d$below_limit, d$limit_rate, d$sgr)
  gauss <- gaussian(link = "identity")
  switch(convention,
    measured = bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "decline"), data = d, family = gauss),
    floored  = bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "decline"), data = transform(d, y = pmax(y, 0)),
                    family = gauss),
    deleted  = bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "decline"), data = d[d$y >= 0, ],
                    family = gauss),
    # Zero for a measured negative; the counting limit for a row that fell below
    # it, which is what the test actually establishes about that row.
    censored = bnec(y | cens(cens) ~ crf(dose, "decline"),
                    data = transform(d,
                      cens = ifelse(below_limit | y < 0, "left", "none"),
                      y = ifelse(below_limit, limit_rate, pmax(y, 0))),
                    family = gauss),
    beta     = bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "decline"),
                    data = transform(d, y = pmax(y, 0) / max(pmax(y, 0))),
                    family = Beta(link = "identity")),
    gamma    = bnec(y ~ crf(dose, "decline"), data = transform(d, y = pmax(y, 0)),
                    family = Gamma(link = "identity")))
}
```

These 24 fits are not run when this vignette is built. Instead, they are run
in the compendium used for the simulation, against the pinned version of
`bayesnec` used there, and their results are transcribed here (Section 9 gives
the time they take and what this means for reading them).

```{r, echo = FALSE}
